              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "doc_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.423,
            0.132,
            0.631,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Summary"
            }
          ],
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.178,
            0.858,
            0.199
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "■ The perimeter of a polygon is the sum of the lengths of all its sides."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.203,
            0.885,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "a. The perimeter of a rectangle is twice the sum of its length and width."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.252,
            0.884,
            0.293
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "b. The perimeter of a square is four times the length of any one of its sides."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.299,
            0.884,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "■ The area of a closed figure is the measure of the region enclosed by the figure."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.345,
            0.633,
            0.365
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "■ Area is generally measured in square units."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.368,
            0.884,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "■ The area of a rectangle is its length times its width. The area of a square is the length of any one of its sides multiplied by itself."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.413,
            0.884,
            0.456
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "■ Two closed figures can have the same area with different perimeters, or the same perimeter with different areas."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.459,
            0.885,
            0.526
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Areas of regions can be estimated (or even determined exactly) by breaking up such regions into unit squares, or into more generalshaped rectangles and triangles whose areas can be calculated."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.085,
            0.942,
            0.117,
            0.959
          ],
